WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.806 --> 00:00:18.476
Stelfie is a very funny 
and very clumsy dude. 

00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:22.022
He time travels and has 
the most incredible adventures.

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:26.192
And he is sort of 
an alter ego myself

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:28.611
although physically we are 
completely different.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.990
Originally I started this project

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:36.244
as a way to showcase the potential 
of Stable Diffusion

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:39.372
combined with good artist skills.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:42.333
My goal was to capture a scene

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.754
where Stelfie engages in a boxing match 
with Muhammad Ali.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.090
I always start with, you know, 
drawing a sketch.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.844
Stable Diffusion and the other 
diffusion models around

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:56.431
are extremely good, but also 
extremely cheeky.

00:00:56.806 --> 00:01:01.770
It's very easy for them to 
drive you away

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.731
from the original idea 
that you had.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:09.778
Once I have the idea, I want to try 
a bunch of random prompts...

00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:12.697
just to see if I can find

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:15.116
at least a good initial pose.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.453
I couldn't find the pose 
for Stelfie on the right.

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:22.123
So, what I've done is that 
I moved to Photoshop...

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.667
and I recreate the pose myself.

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:27.670
ControlNet an extension

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.216
if I had to reproduce today 
the same pose that...

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:34.344
I've done two months ago 
with this artwork...

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:37.514
probably would take me 
15 minutes.

00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:40.558
Throughout the whole process

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.312
I always use different samplers.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:46.648
Because the sampler is very important

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:49.400
in terms of realism and details.

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:53.363
So if you're trying to 
replicate skin...

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:57.158
Euler is very synthetic, 
is very fake.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.538
But DPM, for example, is 
working great on that.

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:07.836
There are many parameters that are 
extremely important.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.421
One is steps.

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:15.093
So steps is how many times 
you’re telling Stable Diffusion

00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:16.719
to work on your prompt.

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:19.848
You can choose a very low number 
or a very high number.

00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:21.808
Many options. 

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:25.270
At the beginning, obviously,
the two more important parts

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.106
are the the inpaint and the outpaint.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:32.652
So inpaint means you are asking
the machine to change

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:35.989
just the parts of the image

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:38.616
and the machine will change

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:41.244
only the part that you want it 
to be changed.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:46.416
On the other side, outpaint, 
you’re asking to the machine...

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:50.962
to imagine what's outside the box

00:02:50.962 --> 00:02:54.340
based on what is already in the box.

00:02:55.091 --> 00:02:57.218
As you can see, I look back 
a lot between 

00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:58.887
Stable Diffusion and Photoshop.

00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:01.139
So let's say that's out of 100%.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:03.391
50% is done with Stable Diffusion

00:03:03.391 --> 00:03:05.935
about 40% in Photoshop

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:08.771
and about 10% in Procreate.

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:16.905
When it comes to Stelfie’s face...

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:22.035
I use a model that has been trained 
specifically on Stelfie’s face.

00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:26.539
So what I've done previously is that 
I created Stelfie in 3D...

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:29.375
and I took a bunch of snapshots...

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:31.794
of his face from different angles...

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:34.380
and I use those images 
to train the model.

00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:36.674
So when you train the model

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:38.301
you save a keyword.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.311
The noise strength is the last option

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:51.314
that you can see in the web UI 
of Stable Diffusion.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.107
And why is important?

00:03:53.107 --> 00:03:56.861
Because it gives you more or less control...

00:03:57.278 --> 00:03:59.447
on the image itself.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:04.869
When it comes to faces, 
it’s very difficult to achieve...

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:06.412
good results.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:12.460
Especially if you're trying to 
replicate a popular person.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.587
So I've asked Stable Diffusion

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:17.465
to make a face that would 
look like Muhammad Ali.

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:22.178
And then in Photoshop, 
I warped all these traits.

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:25.682
So I make the nose — I don't know, 
larger or thinner.

00:04:25.974 --> 00:04:27.600
And the jaw...

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.520
the eyes.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.939
So, I changed everything manually.

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.900
But what is important now 
is to try to have the pose

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:36.985
that is correct.

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:39.028
And I wanted to—

00:04:39.028 --> 00:04:41.823
I wanted Stelfie to be like 
a bit fluffy

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:45.076
in terms of not super fit,
without a six pack.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.329
And probably here I sort of found...

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.165
the belly that I was happy with.

00:04:51.332 --> 00:04:55.336
But clearly it was not realistic enough yet.

00:04:59.924 --> 00:05:01.509
And then I used the result

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:03.678
and I modified the result in Photoshop.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.264
But my understanding

00:05:06.264 --> 00:05:08.975
of how Muhammad Ali 
was looking like.

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:11.728
So you have to, you know

00:05:11.728 --> 00:05:15.815
and crop and cut and paste and 
change and warp and paint

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:19.485
on top, arms and eyes, 
exposure, skin tone

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.779
so a lot a lot of stuff.

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:26.117
So here I was about halfway done.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.747
But then eventually, if you're 
running into trouble...

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:33.458
even if you're not super precise...

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:36.502
you can, you know, go back in 
the Stable Diffusion

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:40.673
and try to ask Stable Diffusion 
to help here.

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:44.802
You, you know, just maybe on 
some part of some edges

00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:47.805
or with the lighting or 
with the skin.

00:05:47.805 --> 00:05:49.265
This sort of...

00:05:49.891 --> 00:05:50.725
stuff.

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:54.812
Still the the body of Muhammad Ali 
is not correct.

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:06.282
He wasn't buff like 
the athletes are today.

00:06:06.282 --> 00:06:09.118
You know, he was big 
and healthy.

00:06:09.118 --> 00:06:13.456
But not  like this muscle 
that they have today.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:14.332
Very defined.

00:06:15.124 --> 00:06:18.252
And so I wanted to get that real...

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:21.672
realistic Muhammad Ali.

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:24.801
Here we are in a good position

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:28.304
because probably we are 
12 or 14 hours in.

00:06:28.805 --> 00:06:30.932
And as you can see, the scene is set.

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:33.184
So it's all good to go.

00:06:33.184 --> 00:06:35.895
You just need to make it better.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:36.729
Well here—

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.690
Probably they don't like nipples, 
who knows.

00:06:40.525 --> 00:06:42.610
The nipples period of Stelfie.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.946
You know, before December

00:06:44.946 --> 00:06:47.115
which is when Stelfie was born...

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:49.117
I was going to bed

00:06:49.117 --> 00:06:51.744
and there was dreaming about
beautiful ladies.

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:57.041
Now all that I dream about 
is about 41 years old dude.

00:06:57.291 --> 00:07:00.086
So, no. Not great. 
Not great.

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:04.715
Well, I feel you have to
drive the machine

00:07:04.715 --> 00:07:05.967
not the other way around.

00:07:06.342 --> 00:07:09.220
And just to prove how important 

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:12.640
is the artist’s part in the overall 
process creation.

00:07:13.307 --> 00:07:15.476
I see the overall process

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:18.354
as a joint effort with the AI.

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:22.692
I’ve been a traditional artist for 2 decades,
painting on canvas.

00:07:22.692 --> 00:07:23.901
And in the last five years

00:07:23.901 --> 00:07:26.070
I've been doing a lot of digital art.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:29.657
So from that part of myself, 
I don't feel threatened at all.

00:07:29.991 --> 00:07:31.993
I feel this is an opportunity.

00:07:31.993 --> 00:07:35.872
An opportunity for many 
new talented people...

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:39.375
to jump on a new branch of art

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:41.461
that is completely different

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:44.380
from the one that we have already 
in digital art

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.717
and just open up new way 
of being creative.

00:07:55.266 --> 00:08:00.605
50% of the hands that you see 
in Stelfies are mine.

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:03.399
And that's because it has always been

00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:06.319
extremely challenging to 
reproduce hands.

00:08:06.777 --> 00:08:11.449
So what I was doing is that, you know, 
if I needed the hand in some position

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:14.076
I would take a picture of my hand...

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:17.538
and then I would clean it up 
and paste on top.

